! DF -- the DATA FETCH address alarm (indicator 20), mask-zero case.
!
! SUPERSEDED AGAIN, and this time by an implementation: THE
! MASK-DEPENDENT HALF IS NOW BUILT for p.32's class 1, and
! programs/df_fetch_suppress.strap tests it at all four sites in all
! three of 8C's conditions (disabled/mask 1, enabled/mask 0,
! enabled/mask 1).  Three claims below are therefore FALSE as written
! and are left in place only so the correction is visible: "this model
! has no termination mechanism at all", "a mask-one case here would
! complete the operation and then interrupt", and "nothing here could
! pass for the right reason".  What remains true is this program's own
! scope -- it runs the mask-ZERO case -- and the reason it is still
! worth running: the INDICATOR half, which is mask-independent, and the
! DS/DF split at the read-modify-write stores.
!
! Also corrected since: p.32's "terminated" is the storage-protection
! chapter speaking loosely.  Figure 11 (printed p.43) gives DF class
! "P,S*,C" with "*Class S when mask is one; otherwise Class C", so
! mask-one DF is SUPPRESSED, not terminated.  See dfSuppresses in
! StretchExecute.bsv.
!
!
! SUPERSEDED 2026-09-24, AND THE CORRECTION MATTERS MORE THAN THE
! PROGRAM.  The "weaker standard" note above rests on the claim that no
! listing here tests DF.  THAT CLAIM IS FALSE.  jb_ax5b routine 1260
! TEST 8C -- PDF pp.76-83, octal 022546.00-022732.40, labels
! 160127-160146 -- is a dedicated test of the DF AND IF INDICATORS
! under four enable/mask conditions, verified first-hand at 400 dpi:
!
!   160127 LVI,$X1,160AD1  -TEST 8C CHECKS DF + IF UNDER 4 CONDS,
!                          -1. DISABL MASK 0 FETCH OK IND NO INT
!                          -2. DIAABL MASK 1 FETCH OK NO IND NO INT
!                          -3. ENABLE MASK 0 FETCH OK IND ON NO INT
!                          -4. ENABLE MASK 1 NO FETCH IND ON INTRPT
!
! Condition 4 is the MASK-DEPENDENT half these programs scope out.  So
! this file was written to a weaker standard than was available, in a
! document this project had already read, thirty pages before the
! routine whose misreading produced the claim.  The program is not
! thereby wrong -- but it is not the strongest evidence available, and
! TEST 8C is the acceptance test it should be checked against.  See
! docs/NEXT.md item 2.
! WEAKER STANDARD, and stated as such.  Nothing in the listings this
! project has read tests DF.  jb_ax5b routine 1268 looked like the
! oracle and is not: its header is "1268 - IF AND DF CLOSE TO DS" and
! its error messages are "LOADED OLD DATA FROM EXT MEM." and "EXECUTED
! OLD INSTRUCTION FROM EXT." -- the wrong VALUE arriving, not an
! indicator failing to set (compare routine 1260's "UNABLE TO TURN AD
! ON.").  DF and IF there are the data-fetch and instruction-fetch
! PATHS, and "close to DS" is close to a Data Store.  Both halves of
! 1268 are transcribed and run elsewhere in this suite; neither is an
! oracle for this indicator.  So this program is hand-written from
! Reference Manual p.32, exactly as storage_protection.strap was
! written before ax5b_16086_ds.strap gave DS a deck.
!
! THE RULE, p.32 verbatim: "When an address alarm is caused by a data
! address used in a fetch-type operation, indicator DF is turned on.
! If the DF mask bit is one, the data fetch is suppressed and the
! operation terminated.  If the DF mask bit is zero, the operation
! proceeds normally.  A fetch-type operation is one in which the
! contents of an addressed storage location are read out for use but
! are not subject to change."
!
! AND THE ENABLE GATE, same page: "When the interruption system is
! disabled, address monitoring is ineffective... the DS, DF and IF
! indicators cannot be turned on when the system is disabled."
!
! THIS PROGRAM RUNS THE MASK-ZERO CASE ONLY, which is a scope
! statement and not an omission left implicit.  With the mask bit zero
! the machine sets DF and carries on -- both halves observable, and
! both checked below.  With the mask bit ONE the machine must SUPPRESS
! the fetch and TERMINATE the operation, and this model has no
! termination mechanism at all: protViolates' comment in
! StretchExecute.bsv has said so since DS was written ("this
! suppresses only the store, so a BB whose write is suppressed still
! branches").  A mask-one case here would complete the operation and
! then interrupt, so nothing here tests it, because nothing here could
! pass for the right reason.
!
! IT IS CLASS 1 OF p.32'S THREE.  "Address monitoring for Data Fetch
! includes: 1. The effective address of all fetch-type operations with
! a direct address.  2. The effective address at each level of the LOAD
! VALUE EFFECTIVE operation with the exception of the final effective
! address which is loaded into the specified index register.  3. The
! refill address used in index refill operations and the operations
! REFILL and REFILL ON COUNT ZERO."  LVE is out of scope in this model
! (dirIndexOp's default arm decodes it Operation Code Invalid), so
! class 2 is moot.  Class 3, the refill addresses, is NOT implemented
! and NOT tested here -- the model reads a refill word at seven sites
! and none of them signals.
!
! WHAT SEPARATES DF FROM DS, and why half of this program is stores.
! A read-modify-write store -- VFL ST, direct-index SV -- READS its
! word before writing it, and this model's single read path serves
! fetches and stores alike.  p.32 puts that read on the STORE side: a
! fetch-type operation is one whose contents "are not subject to
! change", which a store's word plainly is.  So every store below must
! raise DS and leave DF alone, and a model that signalled DF wherever
! it issued a read would fail at exactly those three lines.
!
! THE MASK BIT IS STORED rather than left at its reset value.  p.37:
! bits 20-47 of the mask register "can be set to either zero or one by
! storing the desired value in these positions".  This model resets the
! mask to FFFFF000.00000000, so bit 20 is zero already, and a check
! that leaned on that would be testing the reset value, not the rule.
! Word 12 is in p.31's 0-31 carve-out, so storing it never signals.
!
! Instructions: BD, BE, LVI, SV, L(BU), K(BU), ST(BU), L(N), LV,
! CT0110, BB, BB1, BBZ, BZB, B, BEW -- all implemented.
!
! NOTE THE MODIFIER, because it picks the instruction FORMAT and this
! program needs both.  "L(U)" is the half-word FLOATING-POINT load
! (U = unnormalized); "L(BU,64,8)" is the full-word VFL load.  They go
! down different arms of the model and each needs its own check.

        SLC, (8)4100.0
START   BD,  $+.32                   ! disable: words 2 and 3 are protected
        LVI, $X13, TBL
        SV,  $X13, $IA
        LVI, $X13, WLOW
        SV,  $X13, $LB               ! lower boundary := WLOW
        LVI, $X13, WABOVE
        SV,  $X13, $UB               ! upper boundary := WABOVE
        BBZ, 3.57, $+1.0             ! control bit := 0: signal INSIDE
        BBZ, 12.20, $+1.0            ! DF's mask bit := 0
        BE,  $+.32                   ! monitoring is ineffective disabled
!
! ---- 1. a fetch-type VFL operation, direct address, inside --------
        L(BU,64,8), WLOW
        BZB, $DF,  $                 ! hang if DF did not light
        BB,  $IF,  $                 ! hang if it lit the NEIGHBOURING bit
                                     !   too: DF is 20 and IF is 21, and
                                     !   without this line the injection
                                     !   that swaps them is indistinguish-
                                     !   able from the one that removes
                                     !   the signal
        BZB, $RGZ, $                 ! hang if the value never arrived:
                                     !   mask zero, so the load proceeds.
                                     !   RGZ is still off here -- nothing
                                     !   above this line sets it, because
                                     !   LVI and SV set the INDEX result
                                     !   indicators (XF/XCZ/XV*), not the
                                     !   accumulator's
        BBZ, $DF,  $+1.0             !   ... clear DF for the next check
!
! ---- 1b. the other two fetch-type VFL codes ----------------------
!   L, + and K are the VFL-format operations that read storage without
!   changing it; a predicate narrowed to L alone would pass check 1.
        K(BU,64,8), WHIGH
        BZB, $DF,  $
        BBZ, $DF,  $+1.0
!
! ---- 2. the same operation OUTSIDE the area ----------------------
        BBZ, $RGZ, $+1.0             ! clear RGZ: check 1 left it ON, and a
                                     !   check that cannot fail is not one
        L(BU,64,8), WBELOW
        BB,  $DF,  $                 ! hang if an unmonitored fetch lit DF
        BZB, $RGZ, $                 ! hang if THAT load did not land either
!
! THE NOPS AFTER EACH STORE ARE NOT PADDING.  DS is permanently masked
! on, so an enabled machine reports a store violation by INTERRUPTING,
! and this model takes an interruption at a QUIESCENT boundary --
! look-ahead empty, E-box idle -- rather than at the instruction
! boundary p.48 names.  StretchCore.bsv states that deviation and says
! it is "LATER, never earlier", and that "the test's instruction stream
! is written so the two conditions coincide".  A VFL ST bails out once
! for its read-modify-write read, so it leaves the E-box busy a call
! longer than the BB-style stores the two storage_protection programs
! use, and one instruction of slack is not enough: measured, the check
! below halted with DS ON in word 11 and word 6 still zero.  The count
! is generous rather than tuned -- a stream that only just works would
! be a test of the timing, which is not what this file is about.
!
! ---- 3. a store inside the area is DS, not DF --------------------
        ST(BU,64,8), WLOW
        NOP
        NOP
        NOP
        NOP
        NOP
        NOP
        NOP
        NOP
        BB,  $DF,  $                 ! hang if a store's own read lit DF
        BZB, 6.0,  $                 ! hang if DS's routine did not run
        BBZ, 6.0,  $+1.0
!
! ---- 4. direct index arithmetic: LV fetches, SV stores -----------
        LV,  $X14, WLOW
        BZB, $DF,  $                 ! hang if a direct-index FETCH missed DF
        BBZ, $DF,  $+1.0
        SV,  $X14, WLOW
        NOP
        NOP
        NOP
        NOP
        NOP
        NOP
        NOP
        NOP
        BB,  $DF,  $                 ! hang if a direct-index STORE lit DF
        BZB, 6.0,  $
        BBZ, 6.0,  $+1.0
!
! ---- 5. the floating-point operand fetch -------------------------
        L(N), WHIGH
        BZB, $DF,  $
        BBZ, $DF,  $+1.0
!
! ---- 6. a connective that only reads -----------------------------
!   CONNECT FOR TEST, p.76: the result "is not placed anywhere", so it
!   is fetch-type by p.32's own definition.  CONNECT TO MEMORY is not,
!   and is not used here.
        CT0110(BU,64), WHIGH
        BZB, $DF,  $
        B,   PASS
PASS    BEW, $
!
! ---- the monitored area and its neighbours -----------------------
        SLC, (8)4300.0
WBELOW  DD(BU,64,8), (8)0000125252525252525240   ! below the lower boundary
WLOW    DD(BU,64,8), (8)0000125252525252525240   ! == the lower boundary
WHIGH   DD(BU,64,8), 0                           ! inside the area
WABOVE  DD(BU,64,8), 0                           ! == the upper boundary
!
! ---- the interruption table --------------------------------------
!   Only DS interrupts here: it is permanently masked on (p.48), and
!   DF's mask bit is zero by the store above.  The marker is word 6 for
!   the reason storage_protection_boundary.strap gives -- it is in the
!   0-31 carve-out and cannot itself signal.
        SLC, (8)4500.0
TBL     DD, 0                        ! entry N is at TBL+N words
        SLC, (8)4523.0
DSRTN   BB1, 6.0, $+1.0              ! entry 19 = DS
        END, START
